    "custom_html_cDAb6z": {
      "type": "custom-html",
      "settings": {
        "code": "<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Blog written by Angry Orange.</p>\n<p>Your dog is happy. You’re happy. But once they get a little too happy, BOOM – puddle on the floor.</p><p> \nSo if you’re wondering, “Why does my dog pee when she’s excited?” Don’t worry —we’ve got the fix. While excitement and submissive urination are real (and really annoying), we’ve got the solution to stop the leaks, crash the surprise pee parties, and keep your home smelling fresh.</p><p>\n</p><h3>Step 1: Manage Excitement Urination</h3>\nSome dogs just get too excited. Their joy turns into an uncontrollable sprinkler system. Here’s how to shut it down:\n<ul>\n<li>Keep greetings chill. When you walk in, act like you’re too cool to care. No over-the-top hugs, no high-pitched squeals—just a casual \"Hey, bud.\"</li>\n<li>Ignore your dog for the first few minutes after coming home. It’s not mean, it’s strategic. Let them settle before you engage.</li>\n<li>Pre-game before playtime. Before grabbing the leash or tossing the ball, make them sit. Reward calm behavior, not bouncing-off-the-walls energy.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Step 2: Boost Their Confidence (No Wimpy Pups Allowed!)</h3>\nIf your dog pees when nervous, it’s time for a confidence boost. Here’s how to toughen them up—without the drill sergeant routine:\n\n<ul>\n<li>Pawsitive vibes only. No yelling, no punishment. Reward the good behavior, and ignore the bad.</li>\n<li>Hype them up when they stay calm. If they hold it together in an exciting situation, let them know they nailed it.</li>\n<li>Ditch the scary body language. Crouch instead of towering over them, and ease up on the intense eye contact. You’re their buddy, not an interrogator.</li>\n</ul><p></p>",
        "layout": "t4s-container-wrap",
        "cl_bg": "",
        "cl_bg_gradient": "",
